        <div class="sub-panel" id="recruitment">
          <div class="section-header">
            <div>
              <span class="ctx">02 · Framework</span>
              <h2>Recruitment process</h2>
              <div class="tagline">Ensures capacity is built ahead of, not behind, growth requirements</div>
            </div>
            <span class="pill">Assessment rounds</span>
          </div>

          <p class="lead">The recruitment framework defines what the Field Sales Agent role looks like and attracts the right talent through a consistent EVP, clear agent profiles, defined talent pools, and standardized assessment processes.</p>

          <div class="subsection">
            <div class="subsection-title">Core elements</div>
            <div class="skeleton-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);">
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="evp"><h3>EVP</h3><p>The Employee Value Proposition defines <b>what Field Sales agents can expect from Teya and plays a key role in attracting and retaining the right talent</b>.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="agent-jd"><h3>Agent profile &amp; job description</h3><p>Clear definition of role requirements, qualifications, and performance expectations.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="talent-pool"><h3>Talent pool &amp; sourcing</h3><p>Definition of candidate segments, sourcing channels and preparation of funnel strategy.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="assessment-process"><h3>Assessment process</h3><p><b>Standardised hiring process running across three rounds</b>, each assessing candidates progressively.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="evaluation-framework"><h3>Evaluation framework</h3><p><b>Standardised scorecards in Ashby</b> to evaluate candidates consistently across all interview rounds.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>


        <div class="sub-panel" id="onboarding">
          <div class="section-header">
            <div>
              <span class="ctx">03 · Framework</span>
              <h2>Onboarding</h2>
              <div class="tagline">Drives consistency and speed-to-productivity from day one</div>
            </div>
            <span class="pill">Onboarding phases</span>
          </div>

          <div class="lead-block">
            <p>The Field Sales onboarding journey is a <b>standardized, cross-country program designed to provide a clear and consistent path to productivity for new joiners</b>. It replaces fragmented local approaches with a <b>structured 16-week journey that combines training, field exposure, manager coaching, and a defined learning path</b>.</p>
            <p>The program addresses key challenges such as inconsistent ramp-up, slow time-to-productivity, and uneven field readiness, while establishing common performance standards across markets. Its goal is to <b>help new hires become productive faster, build confidence early, and reduce new hire turnover</b> through clearer expectations, faster confidence building, and earlier intervention where there is poor fit or capability gaps.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="subsection">
            <div class="subsection-title">Key principles</div>
            <div class="skeleton-grid">
              <div class="skel-card"><h3>Blended learning</h3><p>Structured onboarding starts with classroom and virtual training, then quickly shifts to field-based practice.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card"><h3>Cohort-based onboarding</h3><p>Cohort-based start dates (typically every second week) ensure consistent delivery and peer learning, while flexible paths allow for off-cycle joiners.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card"><h3>Prioritizing what drives results</h3><p>Focus early on lead indicators such as activity quality, pipeline development, and conversion signals &mdash; not just closed deals.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card"><h3>Building skills through practice</h3><p>Capabilities are built progressively through repetition and real selling, combining training, practice, feedback, and continuous improvement, with complexity added gradually over time.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card"><h3>Evidence over self-reporting</h3><p>Progress is assessed through observable performance (KPIs, simulations, field activity), not self-reporting.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card"><h3>Managers as enablers</h3><p>Managers play an active role through weekly shadow visits, regular 1:1s, and ongoing coaching &mdash; ensuring early feedback, faster development, and timely intervention when needed.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="frame-subsection">
            <h3>16-week schedule &amp; success metrics</h3>
            <p class="frame-sub-intro">The 16-week onboarding program <b>starts on the first working day</b>, with immediate integration into training, tools, and the team environment. The schedule progressively shifts from structured learning to independent field execution: <b>Week 1 is ~85% training</b>, <b>Weeks 2&ndash;3 are a balanced split</b>, and <b>from Week 4 onwards agents spend four days a week in the field, with training limited to Mondays</b>. Early weeks focus on building foundations and confidence, while later stages emphasize performance, consistency, and ownership of results. <b>Each stage is anchored in clearly defined weekly success metrics</b>, ensuring progress is measurable and expectations are transparent throughout the journey.</p>
            <div class="onb-timeline">
              <div class="onb-phase"><div class="ph-week">Week 1</div><h5>Foundations</h5><p>~85% structured training. Tools, product, sales method, team integration.</p></div>
              <div class="onb-phase"><div class="ph-week">Weeks 2&ndash;3</div><h5>Balanced split</h5><p>50/50 training and field practice. First shadow visits and supported calls.</p></div>
              <div class="onb-phase"><div class="ph-week">Weeks 4&ndash;8</div><h5>Ramp-up</h5><p>4 days field, 1 day training (Mondays). Activity quality and early conversion.</p></div>
              <div class="onb-phase"><div class="ph-week">Weeks 9&ndash;12</div><h5>Performance</h5><p>Full field cadence. Pipeline development, consistent conversion, deeper coaching.</p></div>
              <div class="onb-phase"><div class="ph-week">Weeks 13&ndash;16</div><h5>Ownership</h5><p>Independent execution. Target ownership and graduation to standard rhythm.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="frame-subsection">
            <h3>Learning platform: Sana</h3>
            <p class="frame-sub-intro"><b>Sana is the central onboarding platform</b>, combining learning, practice, and performance tracking into a structured, scalable, and trackable experience. It includes:</p>
            <table class="modal-table">
              <tr><td class="col-narrow">Learning journey</td><td>Personalised onboarding path with live sessions and self-paced courses</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="col-narrow">Assessments</td><td>Knowledge checks and quizzes with follow-up recommendations</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="col-narrow">Practice &amp; application</td><td>Exercises, role-plays (AI + live), and field debriefs</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="col-narrow">Performance reflection</td><td>Monthly submission of KPIs, learnings, and development plans</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="col-narrow">Tracking &amp; oversight</td><td>Progress tracking giving managers full visibility to identify gaps early and intervene</td></tr>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="frame-subsection">
            <h3>Governance</h3>
            <p class="frame-sub-intro">The <b>core syllabus is defined centrally and translated locally</b>. Local teams create market-specific modules, organise live sessions, assign trainers, and schedule field visits.</p>
          </div>
        </div>


        <div class="sub-panel" id="commission">
          <div class="section-header">
            <div>
              <span class="ctx">04 · Framework</span>
              <h2>Commission model</h2>
              <div class="tagline">Aligns incentives with NPV creation, not simply volume</div>
            </div>
            <span class="pill">Commission components</span>
          </div>

          <div class="lead-block">
            <p>Each agent's <b>compensation is structured as OTE (On-Target Earnings) &mdash; a base salary plus a variable commission component</b> &mdash; with both figures held in Hibob as the source of truth for role, base pay, and variable potential. <b>Agents are paid on achievement versus monthly targets across a defined set of components</b>, with targets uploaded each month by the OP&amp;A team and results calculated by the commission engine against live merchant data at the store level.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="frame-subsection">
            <h3>The model at a glance</h3>
            <div class="commission-block">
              <h4>Three components driving variable pay</h4>
              <table class="modal-table">
                <tr><th>Component</th><th>Weight</th><th>What it measures</th></tr>
                <tr><td><b>M1 Net Revenue</b></td><td>50%</td><td>Actual NR in the customer's first month vs target.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><b>M1 TPV</b></td><td>20%</td><td>Total Payment Volume processed by the cohort in Month 1 vs target.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><b>6M Rolling Net Revenue</b></td><td>30%</td><td>Rolling NR vs cohort book target over six months.</td></tr>
              </table>
            </div>
            <div class="commission-block">
              <h4>Eligibility Gate</h4>
              <p>Must achieve <b>&ge; 70% of target M1 NR</b> to unlock any commission payments.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="commission-block">
              <h4>Accelerator</h4>
              <p>Applied to overall commission performance &mdash; rewards overperformance above target.</p>
              <table class="modal-table">
                <tr><th>Band</th><th>Multiplier</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Below Gate</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
                <tr><td>70%&ndash;100%</td><td>1.0&times;</td></tr>
                <tr><td>100%&ndash;120%</td><td>1.1&times;</td></tr>
                <tr><td>120%&ndash;140%</td><td>1.6&times;</td></tr>
                <tr class="highlight"><td><b>140%&ndash;160%</b></td><td><b>2.0&times;</b></td></tr>
                <tr class="highlight"><td><b>160%&ndash;200%</b></td><td><b>3.0&times;</b></td></tr>
                <tr class="highlight"><td><b>200%&ndash;250%</b></td><td><b>4.0&times;</b></td></tr>
                <tr class="highlight"><td><b>250%+</b></td><td><b>5.0&times;</b></td></tr>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>


        <div class="sub-panel" id="routines">
          <div class="section-header">
            <div>
              <span class="ctx">05 · Framework</span>
              <h2>Sales routines</h2>
              <div class="tagline">Creates rhythm, accountability, and sustained focus on priorities</div>
            </div>
            <span class="pill">Eight routines</span>
          </div>

          <div class="lead-block">
            <p>The best sales organisations run on <b>disciplined routines that create rhythm, focus, and sustained performance</b> &mdash; not reactive bursts of activity. Eight best-practice routines are recommended for Teya field sales teams, split between <b>execution-driven activities</b> (revenue-generating) and <b>coaching-focused development</b> (skill-building and long-term capability).</p>
          </div>

          <div class="frame-subsection">
            <h3>Execution-driven routines</h3>
            <p class="frame-sub-intro">Execution-driven routines create a consistent rhythm <b>focused on performance, pipeline, and priorities</b>. They align teams on what needs to be done to hit targets, surface issues early, and ensure accountability at both hub and country level.</p>
            <div class="skeleton-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);">
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="routine-standups"><h3>Stand-ups</h3><p>Daily / 3x week. HUB Leader + all agents in HUB.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="routine-hub-overview"><h3>HUB overview</h3><p>Weekly. HUB Leader + agents (+ Channel Leader 1x a month).</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="routine-country-overview"><h3>Field country overview</h3><p>Monthly. Channel Leader + HUB Leaders.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="routine-planning"><h3>Planning</h3><p>Monthly. Country Leader + Channel Leader + HUB Leaders.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="routine-sales-update"><h3>Sales update</h3><p>Monthly. Country Leader + Channel Leaders + HUB Leaders + all sales teams.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="frame-subsection">
            <h3>Development-focused routines</h3>
            <p class="frame-sub-intro">Development-focused routines <b>build capability through structured coaching and feedback</b>. They ensure continuous improvement by supporting agents with regular 1:1s, in-field coaching, and early intervention where gaps appear.</p>
            <div class="skeleton-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);">
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="routine-agent-1on1"><h3>Agent 1:1s</h3><p>Monthly. HUB Leader + Agent.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="routine-hubleader-1on1"><h3>HUB leader 1:1s</h3><p>2x a month. Channel Leader + HUB Leader.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="routine-shadowing"><h3>Shadowing</h3><p>Weekly. HUB Leader / Channel Leader + Agent.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="frame-subsection">
            <h3>Suggested timing of routines</h3>
            <p class="frame-sub-intro"><b>Routines are structured across the month to create a consistent rhythm</b>, maintain focus on priorities, and drive sustained performance rather than reactive bursts of activity. The following timeline serves as a recommended baseline, while allowing each country to adapt cadence and format to local needs, team structure, and market dynamics.</p>
            <table class="modal-table">
              <tr><th style="width:20%">When</th><th>Routines</th></tr>
              <tr><td><b>Daily / 3x week</b></td><td>Stand-ups (HUB)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><b>Weekly</b></td><td>HUB overview, Shadowing</td></tr>
              <tr><td><b>2x month</b></td><td>HUB leader 1:1s</td></tr>
              <tr><td><b>Monthly</b></td><td>Field country overview, Planning, Sales update, Agent 1:1s</td></tr>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>


        <div class="sub-panel" id="dashboards">
          <div class="section-header">
            <div>
              <span class="ctx">06 · Framework</span>
              <h2>Sales tracking dashboards</h2>
              <div class="tagline">Provides real-time visibility into performance gaps and root causes</div>
            </div>
            <span class="pill">Dashboard views</span>
          </div>

          <div class="lead-block">
            <p>Sales tracking dashboards provide a <b>standardised and transparent view of performance across teams and levels of the organisation</b>. They consolidate key metrics into a <b>single source of truth</b>, enabling leaders and agents to quickly understand progress, identify risks or gaps, and focus on the most important opportunities.</p>
            <p><b>Four views are designed to ensure each level of the organisation accesses the information most relevant</b> to their responsibilities:</p>
          </div>

          <div class="numbered-grid">
            <div class="num-card" data-modal="dashboard-agent">
              <div class="num">1</div>
              <div><h4>Agent view</h4><p>Focuses on the performance of individual sales agents. Allows agents to track their own activities, pipeline, and results.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="num-card" data-modal="dashboard-hub">
              <div class="num">2</div>
              <div><h4>HUB leader view</h4><p>Focuses on hub performance. Allows HUB leaders to monitor team results, compare agent performance, and manage the overall pipeline within their hub.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="num-card" data-modal="dashboard-channel">
              <div class="num">3</div>
              <div><h4>Channel leader view</h4><p>Focuses on a specific sales channel. Allows channel leaders to track results across hubs, evaluate channel effectiveness, and identify opportunities to improve performance.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="num-card" data-modal="dashboard-country">
              <div class="num">4</div>
              <div><h4>Country leader view</h4><p>Focuses on overall country-level sales performance. Allows country leaders to monitor country results, compare performance across channels and hubs, and support strategic decision-making.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="frame-subsection">
            <h3>Sales KPIs are structured into five categories</h3>
            <p class="frame-sub-intro">Each category provides a comprehensive and balanced view of performance &mdash; covering both results and the underlying drivers.</p>
            <div class="skeleton-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);">
              <div class="skel-card"><h3>Sales performance</h3><p>Overall results across acquisition, activation, revenue, and unit economics.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card"><h3>Pipeline overview</h3><p>Size, value, and progression of opportunities across the pipeline and territories.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card"><h3>Conversion metrics</h3><p>How effectively leads and opportunities move through the funnel and where deals are lost.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card"><h3>Territory management</h3><p>Market coverage, client distribution, and revenue performance across territories.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card"><h3>Headcount status &amp; planning</h3><p>Team capacity, productivity, and hiring needs to support growth targets.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>


        <div class="sub-panel" id="method">
          <div class="section-header">
            <div>
              <span class="ctx">07 · Framework</span>
              <h2>Sales method</h2>
              <div class="tagline">Standardizes how value is communicated across the organization</div>
            </div>
            <span class="pill">Five selling steps</span>
          </div>

          <div class="lead-block">
            <p>The Teya Way of Selling is the <b>standard sales methodology used across all field sales teams</b>. It provides a <b>structured, repeatable approach to every merchant interaction</b> &mdash; from the first conversation to closing and beyond. Mastery of this method is the foundation of consistent performance at every level.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="frame-subsection">
            <h3>The Teya Selling Framework</h3>
            <p class="frame-sub-intro">Every merchant interaction follows a five-step cycle. Each step builds on the last, and the cycle repeats as the relationship deepens.</p>
            <div class="skeleton-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);">
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="sm-gain-access"><h3>1. Gain Access</h3><p>Identify the right contact and secure the conversation. Credibility and a strong opening are critical here.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="sm-engage"><h3>2. Engage</h3><p>Build rapport and earn the right to explore needs. Use open-ended questions to understand the merchant's situation.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="sm-create"><h3>3. Create and Present</h3><p>Design a solution that directly addresses the merchant's needs. Present it as relevant, not generic.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="sm-confirm"><h3>4. Confirm Commitment</h3><p>Gain agreement through trial closes and resolve any objections before asking for the decision.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="sm-relationship"><h3>5. Customer Relationship</h3><p>Activate, support, and grow the merchant. Every closed deal is the start of a long-term relationship.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>


        <div class="sub-panel" id="process">
          <div class="section-header">
            <div>
              <span class="ctx">08 · Framework</span>
              <h2>Sales process &amp; tools</h2>
              <div class="tagline">Defines how work gets done and removes friction through systems</div>
            </div>
            <span class="pill">Pipeline stages</span>
          </div>

          <p class="lead">Currently, two main setups exist for sales processes and tools across markets. Block A follows an end-to-end CRM-based process where the full funnel is tracked in HubSpot. Block B operates without a CRM, relying on spreadsheets and local tools. This structure is temporary and will be replaced with a unified approach through the new Sales App rollout from June 2026.</p>

          <div class="subsection">
            <div class="subsection-title">Pipeline stages</div>
            <div class="stages">
              <div class="stage"><div class="step">01</div><div class="name">Initial contact</div></div>
<div class="stage"><div class="step">02</div><div class="name">Needs analysis</div></div>
<div class="stage"><div class="step">03</div><div class="name">Proposal</div></div>
<div class="stage"><div class="step">04</div><div class="name">Negotiation</div></div>
<div class="stage"><div class="step">05</div><div class="name">Closed won</div></div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="subsection">
            <div class="subsection-title">Core elements</div>
            <div class="skeleton-grid">
              <div class="skel-card"><h3>HubSpot end-to-end CRM process</h3><p>Block A tracks sales funnel end-to-end with leads created directly and deal progression managed through defined stages</p></div>
<div class="skel-card"><h3>Deal tracking mandate</h3><p>All deals must be tracked in HubSpot; if it is not in the system, it does not exist</p></div>
<div class="skel-card"><h3>Stage progression rules</h3><p>Deals are only moved forward based on real actions and defined triggers, not assumptions</p></div>
<div class="skel-card"><h3>Mandatory field definitions</h3><p>Each stage requires specific data inputs ensuring consistency and data quality throughout the funnel</p></div>
<div class="skel-card"><h3>Activity logging discipline</h3><p>All activities must be logged within 48 hours; calls, visits, and outcomes recorded as soon as possible</p></div>
<div class="skel-card"><h3>Non-HubSpot spreadsheet approach</h3><p>Block B relies on spreadsheets and local tools for lead management with less structured early funnel tracking</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>


        <div class="sub-panel" id="dna">
          <div class="section-header">
            <div>
              <span class="ctx">09 · Framework</span>
              <h2>Team DNA</h2>
              <div class="tagline">Builds and sustains the culture of energy, discipline, and ownership</div>
            </div>
            <span class="pill">Five cultural pillars</span>
          </div>

          <div class="lead-block">
            <p><b>Team DNA defines the identity, culture, and operating standards</b> of the Field Sales team.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="subsection">
            <div class="subsection-title">Core elements</div>
            <div class="skeleton-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);">
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="dna-values"><h3>Values &amp; behaviours</h3><p>The values that guide how Field Sales agents show up every day, and the daily behaviours that bring them to life.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="dna-ways"><h3>Ways of working</h3><p>The principles and operating standards that translate values into how the team plans, decides, and executes.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="dna-rituals"><h3>Rituals &amp; celebrations</h3><p>Recurring moments &mdash; from first deals to monthly recognitions and Connect events &mdash; that reinforce culture.</p></div>
              <div class="skel-card" data-modal="dna-identity"><h3>Identity &amp; branding</h3><p>Visual identity, language, and team symbols that make the Field Sales team recognisable and proud.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>


        <div class="sub-panel" id="workplace">
          <div class="section-header">
            <div>
              <span class="ctx">10 · Framework</span>
              <h2>Workplace, tech &amp; infrastructure</h2>
              <div class="tagline">Enables teams to perform efficiently and consistently at scale</div>
            </div>
            <span class="pill">Four operational categories</span>
          </div>

          <div class="lead-block">
            <p>A clear and consistent operational setup is critical to enable Field Sales agents to perform effectively from day one. Without the right workplace environment, tools, systems, and processes in place, productivity is delayed and performance is impacted.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="numbered-grid">
            <div class="num-card" data-modal="wp-setup">
              <div class="num">1</div>
              <div><h4>Workplace setup</h4><p>Defines office access, workspace setup, and local support (e.g. commuting, parking).</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="num-card" data-modal="wp-equipment">
              <div class="num">2</div>
              <div><h4>Equipment &amp; tools</h4><p>Provides required hardware and access to core tools and systems, with standards set centrally and delivered locally.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="num-card" data-modal="wp-travel">
              <div class="num">3</div>
              <div><h4>Business travel</h4><p>Standardizes booking and approval process, with local policy variations.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="num-card" data-modal="wp-expenses">
              <div class="num">4</div>
              <div><h4>Expenses &amp; reimbursements</h4><p>Defines a consistent submission process, with locally adapted rules and thresholds.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="lead-block" style="margin-top: 18px;">
            <p>While most elements in this area are defined <b>locally</b>, reflecting country-specific needs, regulations, and ways of working, a <b>common framework</b> is provided to ensure consistency across markets. In addition, certain components &mdash; particularly systems, tools, and core processes &mdash; are <b>aligned centrally</b> to enable scale, standardization, and a seamless agent experience.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

